crate-jmap-tasks-server/src/lib.rs:

    47: pub use backend::{
    48:     AddedItem, BackendChangesError, BackendSetError, ChangesResult, GetObject, JmapBackend,
    49:     JmapObject, QueryChangesResult, QueryObject, QueryResult, SetError, SetErrorType, SetObject,
    50:     TasksBackend,
    51: };
    ...
    155: pub use jmap_server::ClosureHandler;

backend.rs:10-15:

    pub use jmap_server::{
        AddedItem, BackendChangesError, BackendSetError, ChangesResult, GetObject, JmapBackend,
        JmapObject, QueryChangesResult, QueryObject, QueryResult, SetError, SetErrorType, SetObject,
    };
    pub use jmap_tasks_types::backend::{TaskListProperty, TaskNotificationProperty, TaskProperty};
    pub use jmap_types::PatchObject;

A consumer landing on docs.rs/jmap-tasks-server sees these items in the crate's prelude with no docstring at the re-export site. \`cargo doc\` will link through to the upstream crate, but only the type signature is visible at a glance. A reader cannot tell:

  - Which of these types they should USE in their TasksBackend impl vs which are internal-protocol types.
  - Whether SetError is the wire-format SetError (yes, but no doc) vs a different kind of error.
  - Why ClosureHandler is exposed at the crate root (because consumers may want to register custom handlers via dispatcher.register(method, Arc::new(ClosureHandler::new(...))) — but this is documented nowhere in this crate).

These are re-exports the crate is committed to maintaining (per the api-contract,versioning section of the supplement template, locking the upstream major), but they offer zero help to a consumer.

Recommended fix: every re-export gets a one-line docstring explaining its role IN THIS CRATE. Pattern:

    /// JMAP wire-format SetError; emitted by TasksBackend write methods as
    /// BackendSetError::SetError(...) and serialised into notCreated /
    /// notUpdated / notDestroyed maps by the /set handlers.
    pub use jmap_server::SetError;

    /// Handler-wrapper type used to register custom JMAP method handlers on
    /// a Dispatcher without writing a JmapHandler impl from scratch. Most
    /// consumers should not need this — `register_tasks_handlers` registers
    /// all 14 method names automatically.
    pub use jmap_server::ClosureHandler;

This finding is labelled `opinion` because it is style-grade — the contract is sound, the crate just makes the consumer hunt a bit more than necessary. Filing because the supplement template asks for it (api-contract,docs); willing to drop if the maintainer disagrees.
